WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.773
Picture a small restaurant on a busy Friday night.

2
00:00:05.773 --> 00:00:09.092
On the kitchen wall hangs a board with tonight's plan.

3
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.613
And out front is the room, where the plan really happens.

4
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:16.552
Around seven, a waiter joins two tables for a big group.

5
00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:19.961
A cook takes the fish off, because it ran out.

6
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:22.392
Nobody writes any of it on the board.

7
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.982
By nine, the board and the room tell two different stories.

8
00:00:25.982 --> 00:00:28.160
And the next shift believes the board.

9
00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:29.923
So here is the question.

10
00:00:29.923 --> 00:00:32.583
Who keeps the board and the room in step?

11
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.087
Software has the same problem.

12
00:00:36.087 --> 00:00:42.116
So far, you told the cluster, the machines that run your app, what to run from your laptop.

13
00:00:42.116 --> 00:00:43.507
For one person, fine.

14
00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:46.891
Add colleagues, and it breaks quietly.

15
00:00:46.891 --> 00:00:50.053
A fix by hand at night never reaches the files.

16
00:00:50.053 --> 00:00:54.764
And the last apply wins: fine for a pillow fight, poor for real users.

17
00:00:54.967 --> 00:00:58.734
That gap between the files and what really runs is called drift.

18
00:00:58.734 --> 00:01:03.385
We want one place for every change, and someone who keeps the room equal to it.

19
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.973
Back to the restaurant, with new names.

20
00:01:06.973 --> 00:01:11.355
The board is git, the tool that keeps every saved version of your files.

21
00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:18.549
On the board: a file that sets the greeting of hello-devops, our tiny web app, and how many copies should run.

22
00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:21.717
The room is the cluster, where the app really runs.

23
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:26.380
And the floor manager is a program that lives inside the cluster.

24
00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.432
Here, that program is Argo CD.

25
00:01:28.633 --> 00:01:30.082
Notice the direction.

26
00:01:30.082 --> 00:01:32.705
Nobody phones changes in from outside.

27
00:01:32.705 --> 00:01:35.672
The manager walks over and reads the board.

28
00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:40.125
Then it compares the room with the board, and applies only the difference.

29
00:01:40.125 --> 00:01:45.833
It reads git about every three minutes, and watches the room, so a change there shows up in seconds.

30
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:48.368
Now this idea has earned its name.

31
00:01:48.368 --> 00:01:54.053
Files in git say what should run, and a program keeps the real system matching them.

32
00:01:54.053 --> 00:01:55.068
That is GitOps.

33
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:59.048
And your build pipeline no longer needs a key to the cluster.

34
00:01:59.048 --> 00:02:01.628
It changes a file in git, and that is all.

35
00:02:02.333 --> 00:02:04.691
The manager notes two separate things.

36
00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:07.358
Sync status: does the room match the board?

37
00:02:07.358 --> 00:02:09.777
And health: does the app actually work?

38
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.958
Now play the colleague who fixes things by hand.

39
00:02:12.958 --> 00:02:16.285
Change the greeting in the live cluster, and skip git.

40
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.265
Argo CD runs with its default settings.

41
00:02:19.265 --> 00:02:21.463
What happens to that hand edit?

42
00:02:24.567 --> 00:02:25.600
The edit stays.

43
00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.076
Argo CD sees it within seconds, and only marks the app OutOfSync.

44
00:02:30.076 --> 00:02:33.312
It is still Healthy, because the app runs fine.

45
00:02:33.533 --> 00:02:37.828
Out of the box, Argo CD is a manager who only takes notes.

46
00:02:37.828 --> 00:02:38.716
Very polite.

47
00:02:38.716 --> 00:02:41.233
Not much help at nine on a Friday.

48
00:02:41.867 --> 00:02:44.530
Three switches decide how strict it is.

49
00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.603
Automated sync applies new commits by itself.

50
00:02:47.603 --> 00:02:51.700
Self-heal undoes changes made in the cluster but not in git.

51
00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:54.670
And prune deletes what you removed from git.

52
00:02:54.670 --> 00:03:00.331
Self-heal and prune are off by default, a careful choice for a tool that can delete things.

53
00:03:00.533 --> 00:03:03.596
Turn self-heal on, and watch the hand edit.

54
00:03:03.596 --> 00:03:07.158
After a short pause, Argo CD puts git's text back.

55
00:03:07.158 --> 00:03:08.297
You did nothing.

56
00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:13.861
To change the greeting for good, you change the board: edit the file, commit, and push.

57
00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:15.935
So far so good.

58
00:03:15.935 --> 00:03:17.494
Now let's be unkind.

59
00:03:17.494 --> 00:03:24.195
On Day 43 you added an autoscaler, a controller that adds copies when the app is busy.

60
00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:25.534
A rush comes in.

61
00:03:25.534 --> 00:03:29.363
The autoscaler sets five copies, where git says three.

62
00:03:29.567 --> 00:03:34.645
Self-heal sees a difference from git, and sets it back to three.

63
00:03:34.645 --> 00:03:37.104
The autoscaler raises it again.

64
00:03:37.104 --> 00:03:37.581
Three.

65
00:03:37.581 --> 00:03:37.977
Five.

66
00:03:37.977 --> 00:03:38.453
Three.

67
00:03:38.667 --> 00:03:45.713
They fight as long as the rush lasts: the manager sending home the extra waiters the head waiter just called in.

68
00:03:45.933 --> 00:03:51.446
The lab gives that field one owner: it tells Argo CD to ignore the replica count.

69
00:03:51.446 --> 00:03:54.032
Leaving replicas out of git works too.

70
00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:57.662
Two controllers fighting over one field never stop.

71
00:03:57.662 --> 00:03:59.409
Give each field one owner.

72
00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:07.099
Now a bad change gets into git itself: a typo in the image tag, the app's version name.

73
00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:10.094
Argo CD applies it, so the app is Synced.

74
00:04:10.094 --> 00:04:14.182
But the new copies cannot pull an image that does not exist.

75
00:04:14.182 --> 00:04:15.000
Not Healthy.

76
00:04:15.233 --> 00:04:19.429
You might be thinking: there is a one command undo, from Day 40.

77
00:04:19.429 --> 00:04:20.806
It fixes the cluster.

78
00:04:20.806 --> 00:04:23.232
With self-heal on, what happens next?

79
00:04:26.333 --> 00:04:31.210
The board still says the typo, so Argo CD puts the bad version straight back.

80
00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:35.264
Our manager is loyal to the board, even when the board is wrong.

81
00:04:35.867 --> 00:04:37.544
So a rollback is a commit.

82
00:04:37.544 --> 00:04:42.963
git revert makes a new commit that undoes the bad one, and the history stays intact.

83
00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:43.762
Push it.

84
00:04:43.762 --> 00:04:48.151
Argo CD reads the board, and deploys the state from before.

85
00:04:49.367 --> 00:04:50.526
One honest limit.

86
00:04:50.526 --> 00:04:58.163
In a real emergency, turn off automated sync for that app, fix the cluster by hand, and write down every change.

87
00:04:58.163 --> 00:05:01.845
Then put the same fix into git, and turn sync back on.

88
00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:05.828
Skip step three, and the next sync quietly undoes your fix.

89
00:05:06.633 --> 00:05:08.407
So the moral of the story.

90
00:05:08.407 --> 00:05:11.340
A change that is not in git did not happen.

91
00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:14.478
Git is the board, Argo CD the manager.

92
00:05:14.478 --> 00:05:16.011
One owner per field.

93
00:05:16.011 --> 00:05:17.773
A rollback is a commit.

94
00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:19.387
A quick check.

95
00:05:19.387 --> 00:05:22.238
You delete a file from git, and prune is off.

96
00:05:22.238 --> 00:05:24.963
What happens to that object in the cluster?

97
00:05:28.067 --> 00:05:28.762
It stays.

98
00:05:28.762 --> 00:05:32.859
Argo CD only marks it for pruning, and waits for you.
